<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-chapter-6" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Double Integrals and Applications</title>

<introduction>
  <p>
    In this chapter, we extend integration to functions of two variables.
    Double integrals allow us to compute quantities such as area, volume, mass, and average value
    over regions in the plane. These integrals generalize familiar single-variable ideas, but now require
    careful attention to the geometry of the region being integrated over.
  </p>

  <p>
    We begin by evaluating double integrals as iterated integrals over rectangular regions.
    Fubini's Theorem provides the theoretical foundation for this process and allows us to change
    the order of integration when appropriate. We then move beyond rectangles to more general
    regions, learning how to describe and integrate over nonrectangular domains.
  </p>

  <p>
    The chapter concludes with improper double integrals and a transition to polar coordinates.
    Polar coordinates offer a natural advantage when regions exhibit circular symmetry and often
    simplify both the description of a region and the computation itself. By the end of the chapter,
    you should be comfortable choosing integration methods that best match the geometry of a problem.
  </p>
</introduction>


